window.MONSTER_CHUNK_42 = [{"name":"Divine Guardian Hydra","cr":5,"xp":1600,"alignment":"N","size":"Huge","type":"magical beast","subtypes":["air","water"],"init":5,"senses":[{"type":"darkvision","modifier":"60 ft."},{"type":"low-light vision"},{"type":"scent"}],"ac":15,"ac_touch":9,"ac_flat_footed":14,"ac_mods":"+1 Dex, +6 natural, -2 size","hp":47,"hd":"5d10+20","hdvalue":5,"hp_mods":["fast healing 5"],"fort":8,"ref":5,"will":7,"defensive_abilities":["ability healing"],"immunities":["disease","mind-affecting effects","poison"],"speed":[{"type":"land","speed":40},{"type":"fly","speed":40,"maneuverability":"perfect"},{"type":"swim","speed":40}],"melee":"5 bites +7 (1d8+3)","space":"15 ft.","reach":"10 ft.","special_attacks":[{"type":"pounce"}],"spell_like_abilities_caster_level":5,"spell_like_abilities_concentration":9,"spell_like_abilities":[{"frequency":"at-will","spells":[{"spell":"dimension door","details":"within sacred site only"}]},{"frequency":"1/day","spells":[{"spell":"arcane lock"},{"spell":"augury"},{"spell":"clairvoyance/clairaudience"},{"spell":"hold portal"}]},{"frequency":"3/day","spells":[{"spell":"alarm"},{"spell":"knock"}]}],"strength":17,"dexterity":12,"constitution":18,"intelligence":6,"wisdom":15,"charisma":13,"baseatk":5,"cmb":12,"cmd":27,"feats":[{"feat":"Combat Reflexes"},{"feat":"Iron Will"},{"feat":"Weapon Focus (bite)"}],"skills":[{"skill":"Fly","value":9},{"skill":"Perception","value":16},{"skill":"Sense Motive","value":7},{"skill":"Swim","value":11}],"languages":["Draconic"],"sq":[{"type":"blessed life"},{"type":"divine swiftness"},{"type":"hydra traits"},{"type":"regenerate head"},{"type":"sacred site"}],"environments":["temperate ruins"],"organizations":["solitary"],"treasure":"standard","description_visual":"This regal hydra has one larger head and four smaller ones, and projects an aura of authority and menace.","group":"Divine Guardian","source":"PFRPG Bestiary 4","description":"A divine guardian is a creature chosen by the gods to guard a sacred site of the faith. Blessed with eternal life (or damned, some might say), a divine guardian spends untold centuries in the service of its deity, tirelessly and deathlessly defending its charge from any who would seek to desecrate it. Typically such a creature is transformed into a form more regal than its mortal one, setting it apart from a typical member of its race or species. A divine guardian is spiritually connected to the one site that it must guard for eternity. As long as a divine guardian remains within that site, it doesn't hunger, thirst, get sick, or even age. Within the bounds of its sacred site, a divine guardian possesses numerous defensive powers to ward it from intruders, but it can never leave the area or the long years of its service will finally catch up to it. A divine guardian must weigh the power and prestige of its endless responsibility against the freedom death might inevitably bring. Most divine guardians are chosen servants who agree to willingly serve their gods for all eternity, but some have been cursed with their duty in response for some harm to the god's faithful or as atonement for some great sin. Whatever the nature of its creation, a divine guardian is still beholden to the god that granted it its powers, and to the followers of that god as well. A cleric or paladin of the deity that created a divine guardian can issue the guardian commands. This doesn't give the cleric or paladin complete control over the creature, but the guardian does respond favorably to those requests. For example, a cleric could ask it to not attack her companions, or to help her defend the guardian's sacred site from attackers. A cleric or paladin of the same faith must win an opposed Charisma check to convince a divine guardian to do anything it wouldn't ordinarily do. A divine guardian can never be ordered to leave its sacred site or to go against the tenets of its deity's faith. The divine guardian hydra presented here is built using a hydra use its rules for this creature's hydra traits and regenerate head abilities. This divine guardian hydra is a divine guardian of the god of nature and the weather, thus giving it the air and water subtypes.","ecology":"","habitat_society":""},{"name":"Djinni","cr":5,"xp":1600,"alignment":"CG","size":"Large","type":"outsider","subtypes":["air","extraplanar"],"init":8,"senses":[{"type":"darkvision","modifier":"60 ft."}],"ac":19,"ac_touch":14,"ac_flat_footed":14,"ac_mods":"+4 Dex, +1 dodge, +5 natural, -1 size","hp":52,"hd":"7d10+14","hdvalue":7,"fort":4,"ref":9,"will":9,"immunities":["acid"],"speed":[{"type":"land","speed":20},{"type":"fly","speed":60,"maneuverability":"perfect"}],"melee":"2 slams +10 (1d8+4) or mwk scimitar +11 (1d8+4/18-20)","space":"10 ft.","reach":"10 ft.","special_attacks":[{"type":"air mastery"},{"type":"whirlwind","details":"1/10 minutes, 10-50 ft. tall, 1d8+4 damage, DC 17"}],"spell_like_abilities_caster_level":9,"spell_like_abilities_concentration":15,"spell_like_abilities":[{"frequency":"at-will","spells":[{"spell":"invisibility","details":"self only"},{"spell":"plane shift","details":"willing targets to elemental planes, Astral Plane, or Material Plane only"}]},{"frequency":"1/day","spells":[{"spell":"create food and water"},{"spell":"create wine","details":"as create water, but wine instead"},{"spell":"gaseous form","details":"for up to 1 hour"},{"spell":"major creation","details":"created vegetable matter is permanent"},{"spell":"persistent image"},{"spell":"wind walk"}]}],"strength":18,"dexterity":19,"constitution":14,"intelligence":14,"wisdom":15,"charisma":15,"baseatk":7,"cmb":4,"cmd":18,"feats":[{"feat":"Combat Casting"},{"feat":"Combat Reflexes"},{"feat":"Dodge"},{"feat":"Improved Initiative","type":"bonus"},{"feat":"Wind Stance"}],"skills":[{"skill":"Appraise","value":12},{"skill":"Craft (any one)","value":12},{"skill":"Fly","value":20},{"skill":"Knowledge (planes)","value":12},{"skill":"Perception","value":12},{"skill":"Sense Motive","value":12},{"skill":"Spellcraft","value":12},{"skill":"Stealth","value":10}],"languages":["Aquan","Auran","Common","Ignan","Terran","telepathy 100 ft."],"environments":["any (Plane of Air)"],"organizations":["solitary","pair","company (3-6)","band (7-10)"],"treasure":"standard (masterwork scimitar, other gear)","description_visual":"This creature stands nearly twice as tall as a human, although its lower torso trails away into a vortex of mist and wind.","group":"Genie","source":"PFRPG Bestiary","special_abilities":[{"type":"Air Mastery","kind":"Ex","details":"Airborne creatures take a -1 penalty on attack and damage rolls against a djinni. The djinn (singular djinni) are genies from the Plane of Air. They are said to be made of the stuff of clouds, with the strength of the mightiest storms. A djinni is about 10 ft. tall and weighs about 1,000 pounds."}],"description":"Djinn disdain physical combat, preferring to use their magical powers and aerial abilities against foes. A djinni overmatched in combat usually takes flight and becomes a whirlwind to harass those who follow. When faced with Gelatinous C Gelatinous Cube-Genie, Djinni no other option than melee combat, most djinn prefer to wield masterwork scimitars two-handed. Among other genies, djinn get along well with jann and marids. They are frequently at odds with the shaitans, and are sworn enemies of the efreet, despising these fiery genies more than any other genie race. So legendary is the conf lict between the efreet and the djinn that many spellcasters attempt (to varying degrees of success) to secure a djinni's servitude by promising to aid the cause against their hated enemies. A small percentage of djinn are noble. Noble djinn, often called viziers, have 10 Hit Dice, Strength 23, and Charisma 17, and can grant three wishes to any being (nongenies only) who captures them. Djinn nobles are CR 8.","ecology":"","habitat_society":""},{"name":"Elder Thing","cr":5,"xp":1600,"alignment":"LN","size":"Medium","type":"aberration","subtypes":["aquatic"],"init":2,"senses":[{"type":"all-around vision"},{"type":"darkvision","modifier":"60 ft."}],"ac":18,"ac_touch":12,"ac_flat_footed":16,"ac_mods":"+2 Dex, +6 natural","hp":59,"hd":"7d8+28","hdvalue":7,"fort":8,"ref":4,"will":8,"immunities":["cold"],"resists":[{"type":"fire","value":10}],"speed":[{"type":"land","speed":30},{"type":"fly","speed":20,"maneuverability":"clumsy"},{"type":"swim","speed":40}],"melee":"5 tentacles +7 (1d4+2 plus grab)","space":"5 ft.","reach":"5 ft.","special_attacks":[{"type":"constrict","details":"1d4+2"}],"strength":15,"dexterity":14,"constitution":19,"intelligence":20,"wisdom":16,"charisma":17,"baseatk":5,"cmb":7,"cmb_special":"+4 vs. grapple","cmd":19,"cmd_special":"can't be tripped","feats":[{"feat":"Combat Expertise"},{"feat":"Combat Reflexes"},{"feat":"Endurance"},{"feat":"Great Fortitude"}],"skills":[{"skill":"Disable Device","value":9},{"skill":"Fly","value":4},{"skill":"Heal","value":10},{"skill":"Knowledge (arcana)","value":12},{"skill":"Perception","value":13},{"skill":"Spellcraft","value":15},{"skill":"Survival","value":13},{"skill":"Swim","value":20},{"skill":"Use Magic Device","value":10}],"languages":["Elder Thing"],"sq":[{"type":"amphibious"},{"type":"hibernation"},{"type":"limited starflight"},{"type":"no breath"}],"environments":["any"],"organizations":["solitary","pair","pod (3-8)","expedition (9-16)"],"treasure":"standard","description_visual":"This strange being has a star-shaped head and numerous writhing appendages arranged radially around its barrel-shaped body.","source":"PFRPG Bestiary 4","special_abilities":[{"type":"Hibernation","kind":"Ex","details":"An elder thing can enter a state of hibernation at will; doing so takes 1 minute. While in this state, it can take no actions and is effectively helpless, as if it were in a deep sleep. An elder thing can remain in hibernation for as long as it wishes- while in this state, it doesn't need to eat or drink, nor does it age. Time effectively stands still for a hibernating elder thing. If it is jostled or damaged while hibernating, an elder thing can attempt a DC 20 Will save. If it succeeds, it awakens in 2d4 rounds. Otherwise, it takes 1d4 days to awaken from hibernation. An elder thing can set the length of its hibernation when it first enters this state, so that it can awaken after a set amount of time has passed. When awakening at a set time in this manner, an elder thing needs only 1d3 rounds to rouse itself, with no Will saving throw necessary."},{"type":"Limited Starflight","kind":"Ex","details":"An elder thing can survive in the void of outer space, and its wings allow it to use its fly speed in that environment despite the lack of air. Unlike creatures with full starflight, an elder thing's ability to fly in outer space doesn't allow it to reach unusually high speeds-an elder thing that wishes to travel from one planet to another typically calculates the distance and then hibernates for the majority of the journey, relying on its momentum and inertia to carry it to its destination while it slumbers along the way."}],"description":"Known by various names on various worlds, but most often as \"elder things\" or \"old ones,\" these alien creatures are interested in creating lasting works of art, architecture, and even life (such as the dreaded shoggoths). They possess a boundless capacity for war and egotism. A typical elder thing is 6 ft. tall from head to foot, with a 7-foot wingspan. Surprisingly heavy for its size, an elder thing generally weighs about 450 pounds.","ecology":"","habitat_society":""},{"name":"Embolotherium","cr":5,"xp":1600,"alignment":"N","size":"Large","type":"animal","init":0,"senses":[{"type":"low-light vision"}],"ac":17,"ac_touch":9,"ac_flat_footed":17,"ac_mods":"+8 natural, -1 size","hp":59,"hd":"7d8+28","hdvalue":7,"fort":11,"ref":5,"will":3,"speed":[{"type":"land","speed":30}],"melee":"slam +12 (2d6+12)","space":"10 ft.","reach":"10 ft.","strength":26,"dexterity":11,"constitution":18,"intelligence":2,"wisdom":13,"charisma":3,"baseatk":5,"cmb":14,"cmd":24,"cmd_special":"+4 vs. trip","feats":[{"feat":"Diehard"},{"feat":"Endurance"},{"feat":"Great Fortitude"},{"feat":"Power Attack"}],"skills":[{"skill":"Perception","value":10},{"skill":"Swim","value":12}],"environments":["any plains"],"organizations":["solitary","pair","herd (3-9)"],"treasure":"none","description_visual":"This creature is reminiscent of a rhinoceros, but instead of pointed horns on its nose, this creature possesses a blunt, bony protrusion.","group":"Megafauna","source":"AP 91","description":"Embolotherium are squat, stocky creatures with hollow, bony protrusions on their snouts that are used as sound resonation chambers, allowing the creatures to create very loud noises to communicate across great distances. An average embolotherium stands 5 ft. tall at the shoulder, with a nose-to-tail length of about 8 ft. and a weight of 6,000 pounds.\n\nEmbolotherium are strict herbivores, grazing on grasses and flowering plants. Their eyesight is very poor, but extremely acute hearing makes up for this deficiency. A herd of embolotherium can be quickly provoked into a stampede, and travelers should be wary of even whispered conversation when near a group of these creatures.\n\nNaturally social with others of their kind, embolotherium are generally found in large groups in flat, grassy areas. Their many layers of tough skin serve as deterrents to attack as well as thermal buffers, allowing them to survive in even frigid temperatures as long as there is sufficient food.","ecology":"","habitat_society":""},{"name":"Emperor Cobra","cr":5,"xp":1600,"alignment":"N","size":"Large","type":"animal","init":6,"senses":[{"type":"low-light vision"},{"type":"scent"}],"ac":18,"ac_touch":11,"ac_flat_footed":16,"ac_mods":"+2 Dex, +7 natural, -1 size","hp":51,"hd":"6d8+24","hdvalue":6,"fort":9,"ref":7,"will":5,"speed":[{"type":"land","speed":30},{"type":"climb","speed":30},{"type":"swim","speed":30}],"melee":"bite +10 (2d6+9 plus poison)","space":"10 ft.","reach":"10 ft.","strength":22,"dexterity":15,"constitution":18,"intelligence":1,"wisdom":17,"charisma":2,"baseatk":4,"cmb":11,"cmd":23,"cmd_special":"can't be tripped","feats":[{"feat":"Improved Initiative"},{"feat":"Skill Focus (Stealth)"},{"feat":"Weapon Focus (bite)"}],"skills":[{"skill":"Acrobatics","value":10},{"skill":"Climb","value":14},{"skill":"Perception","value":13},{"skill":"Stealth","value":11},{"skill":"Swim","value":14}],"environments":["temperate swamps","warm swamps"],"organizations":["solitary","pair","nest (3-8)"],"treasure":"none","description_visual":"This massive green cobra rears its head upward aggressively, its brightly colored, scaly hood flaring in an unmistakable warning.","group":"Snake","source":"PFRPG Bestiary 2","special_abilities":[{"type":"Poison","kind":"Ex","details":"Bite-injury; save Fort DC 17; frequency 1/round for 6 rounds; effect 1d3 Con; cure 2 consecutive saves."}],"description":"Infamous among travelers for its deadly poisonous bite, the emperor cobra is a foul-tempered snake typically encountered in bogs. The snake's scales are generally a dark green, with a pale green or even ivory underbelly. Its eyes are bright red and lack the distinctive serpentine slit most snake eyes possess. Emperor cobras are often trained to serve as guardians in temples. They are 16 ft. long and weigh 200 pounds.","ecology":"","habitat_society":""},{"name":"Fachen","cr":5,"xp":1600,"alignment":"NE","size":"Medium","type":"aberration","init":1,"senses":[{"type":"darkvision","modifier":"60 ft."}],"auras":["fear (30 ft., DC 15)"],"ac":18,"ac_touch":14,"ac_flat_footed":14,"ac_mods":"+1 Dex, +3 dodge, +4 natural","hp":45,"hd":"7d8+14","hdvalue":7,"fort":4,"ref":3,"will":8,"speed":[{"type":"land","speed":30}],"melee":"battleaxe +10 (1d8+5/x3), bite +5 (1d6+2)","space":"5 ft.","reach":"5 ft.","strength":20,"dexterity":13,"constitution":15,"intelligence":6,"wisdom":12,"charisma":15,"baseatk":5,"cmb":10,"cmd":24,"cmd_special":"+4 vs. trip","feats":[{"feat":"Acrobatic"},{"feat":"Cleave"},{"feat":"Iron Will"},{"feat":"Power Attack"}],"skills":[{"skill":"Acrobatics","value":13},{"skill":"Perception","value":9},{"skill":"Stealth","value":6}],"languages":["Common"],"sq":[{"type":"incredible balance"}],"environments":["temperate hills","temperate plains"],"organizations":["solitary"],"treasure":"standard (battleaxe)","description_visual":"This half-bodied monstrosity with a toothy mouth and stringy hair hops about on its single leg with unusual grace.","source":"PFRPG Bestiary 4","special_abilities":[{"type":"Fear","kind":"Su","details":"Each creature within a 30-foot radius that sees a fachen must succeed at a DC 15 Will save or be paralyzed by fear for 1d4 rounds. A creature that successfully saves isn't subject to the same fachen's fear aura for 24 hours. This is a paralysis effect and a mind-affecting fear effect. The save DC is Charisma-based."},{"type":"Incredible Balance","kind":"Ex","details":"Despite its strange anatomy, a fachen is adept at keeping itself upright and out of danger. A fachen gains a +3 dodge bonus to AC and a +4 racial bonus to its CMD against trip combat maneuvers."}],"description":"The fearsome fachen's terrifying visage paralyzes all but the most intrepid that happen across its hopping path. Reports disagree on just what it is about the fachen that turns one's blood to ice-the single leg and foot that somehow don't impair the being's balance or speed, the seemingly too-strong arm jutting from the center of the creature's torso, or the lone, bulbous eye leering maliciously at its would-be prey. Most likely it's a combination of all of these things mixed with the unrelenting rage and savagery that seems to ooze from its every pore.\n\nThough the fachen's grotesque approximation of a humanoid form could be enough to scare a traveler witless on its own, no single part of the abomination's appearance induces fear like that of its mouth, a snarling and snapping thing filled with two full rows of long, sharp teeth-a mouth that looks like a cage out of a foul nightmare. Constantly gnashing or chomping in the air, it delivers grievous wounds to those who don't flee this terrible creature. Though they're able to speak, fachens do so only to curse at those they encounter. These barbaric creatures have no discernible culture, and sages and naturalists are still puzzled as to how they even reproduce. It almost seems as if they are pure rage that spontaneous takes on a hideous natural form. A typical fachen stands just short of 6 ft. tall, and despite appearing to be half a person, weighs around 150 pounds because of its muscle mass. Though it isn't a very large creature, its fearsome composition and the aura of fear it exudes give it a presence that makes it seem much greater than its actual size.","ecology":"","habitat_society":""},{"name":"Flame Drake","cr":5,"xp":1600,"alignment":"CE","size":"Large","type":"dragon","subtypes":["fire"],"init":5,"senses":[{"type":"darkvision","modifier":"60 ft."},{"type":"low-light vision"},{"type":"scent"}],"ac":18,"ac_touch":10,"ac_flat_footed":17,"ac_mods":"+1 Dex, +8 natural, -1 size","hp":57,"hd":"6d12+18","hdvalue":6,"fort":8,"ref":6,"will":6,"immunities":["fire","paralysis","sleep"],"weaknesses":["vulnerability to cold"],"speed":[{"type":"land","speed":20},{"type":"fly","speed":60,"maneuverability":"average"}],"melee":"bite +10 (2d6+5 plus 1d6 fire), tail slap +5 (1d6+2)","space":"10 ft.","reach":"10 ft.","special_attacks":[{"type":"fireball breath"}],"strength":21,"dexterity":13,"constitution":16,"intelligence":9,"wisdom":12,"charisma":10,"baseatk":6,"cmb":12,"cmd":23,"feats":[{"feat":"Flyby Attack"},{"feat":"Improved Initiative"},{"feat":"Power Attack"}],"skills":[{"skill":"Fly","value":8},{"skill":"Intimidate","value":9},{"skill":"Perception","value":10},{"skill":"Stealth","value":6},{"skill":"Survival","value":10}],"languages":["Draconic"],"sq":[{"type":"speed surge"}],"environments":["temperate hills","temperate mountains"],"organizations":["solitary","pair","rampage (3-12)"],"treasure":"standard","description_visual":"This ferocious creature looks like a red-and-yellow scaled dragon, but with only two legs.","group":"Drake","source":"PFRPG Bestiary 2","special_abilities":[{"type":"Fireball Breath","kind":"Su","details":"A flame drake can, as a standard action, breathe a ball of flame that explodes like a fireball. This attack has a range of 180 ft. and deals 5d6 points of fire damage (DC 16 Reflex half) to all creatures within a 20-foot-radius spread. Once a flame drake has used its fireball breath, it cannot do so again for 1d6 rounds. The save DC is Constitution-based."},{"type":"Speed Surge","kind":"Ex","details":"Three times per day as a swift action, a flame drake may draw on its draconic heritage for a boost of strength and speed to take an additional move action in that round."}],"description":"Flame drakes are the degenerate cousins of red dragons, with all of the rage and temper of true red dragons but little of the reason and intelligence. Flame drakes are brutal bullies who terrorize the lands they inhabit.\n\nFrom those they can frighten, flame drakes exact harsh tribute, and they viciously attack any not so easily cowed.\n\nFlame drakes gather in small hunting packs called \"rampages,\" working together easily during raids but often falling prey to squabbling and infighting over the spoils. Males and females form their own packs divided by gender, coming together only during the annual mating season.\n\nFlame drakes mate once a year. Males play no part in raising their offspring, and abandon their partners soon after mating. Females lay clutches of two or three eggs in secluded mountain nests, and raise their hatchlings for only 2 years before rejoining their packs and abandoning their offspring to their fates. Flame drakes mature in 5 years, and can live up to 150 years. They generally grow to 12 ft. long and weigh 1,500 pounds.","ecology":"","habitat_society":""},{"name":"Gearghost","cr":5,"xp":1600,"alignment":"CE","size":"Tiny","type":"undead","init":7,"senses":[{"type":"darkvision","modifier":"60 ft."}],"ac":18,"ac_touch":15,"ac_flat_footed":17,"ac_mods":"+3 Dex, +3 natural, +2 size","hp":52,"hd":"7d8+21","hdvalue":7,"fort":5,"ref":7,"will":7,"defensive_abilities":["channel resistance +4","rejuvenation"],"dr":"5/-","immunities":["undead traits"],"speed":[{"type":"fly","speed":40,"maneuverability":"perfect"}],"melee":"2 slams +5 (1d4-2)","space":"2-1/2 ft.","reach":"0 ft.","spell_like_abilities_caster_level":9,"spell_like_abilities_concentration":9,"spell_like_abilities":[{"frequency":"at-will","spells":[{"spell":"detect magic"},{"spell":"mending"},{"spell":"telekinesis"}]},{"frequency":"3/day","spells":[{"spell":"make whole"}]}],"strength":6,"dexterity":17,"intelligence":15,"wisdom":14,"charisma":17,"baseatk":5,"cmb":6,"cmd":14,"feats":[{"feat":"Flyby Attack"},{"feat":"Improved Initiative"},{"feat":"Lightning Reflexes"},{"feat":"Skill Focus (Stealth)"}],"skills":[{"skill":"Craft (traps)","value":17},{"skill":"Disable Device","value":10},{"skill":"Fly","value":20},{"skill":"Knowledge (engineering)","value":9},{"skill":"Perception","value":12},{"skill":"Spellcraft","value":10},{"skill":"Stealth","value":24}],"languages":["Aklo","Common","Undercommon"],"sq":[{"type":"create trap"},{"type":"resetter"}],"environments":["any underground","any urban"],"organizations":["solitary","gang (2-6)"],"treasure":"standard","description_visual":"Grasping arms emerge from a tangled cluster of debris and mechanical parts. A metal skull hovers above the clanging mess.","source":"PFRPG Bestiary 4","special_abilities":[{"type":"Create Trap","kind":"Su","details":"Once per week, a gearghost can create a trap of CR 4 or lower regardless of cost, materials, or skill checks. Two or more gearghosts working together on a trap can increase the maximum CR by 2 for each additional gearghost working in concert. For example, three gearghosts working together can create any CR 8 or lower trap in a week. Example traps can be found in the Core Rulebook."},{"type":"Rejuvenation","kind":"Su","details":"A destroyed gearghost reforms in 2d6 days. To permanently destroy a gearghost, holy water must be poured over its remains within the area of a hallow spell. To complete the destruction, every trap within 100 ft. of the remains must be successfully disabled or destroyed before the gearghost is completely destroyed."},{"type":"Resetter","kind":"Ex","details":"A gearghost can reset traps and keep them repaired with supernatural efficiency. A gearghost can reset a trap with a repair or manual reset as a move action. If a trap has an automatic reset that is longer than immediate, a gear ghost can reset that trap as a free action."}],"description":"Formed from the unquiet soul of a thief wrenched from life by a wicked trap, a gearghost delights in rigging up the same threats and tortures to which it succumbed. Eager to witness the deaths of others, the gearghost prowls the place of its death, constantly maintaining and resetting any traps in that area as it waits for victims to fall into its clutches. A gearghost also has the ability to create and set new traps, and as such its lair is usually choked with perilous new snares, spikes, and pits. A gearghost only keeps treasure as a lure for hapless adventurers, often using such treasure as the trigger for one of its devious creations.","ecology":"","habitat_society":""},{"name":"Genthodaemon","cr":5,"xp":1600,"alignment":"NE","size":"Large","type":"outsider","subtypes":["daemon","evil","extraplanar"],"init":0,"senses":[{"type":"darkvision","modifier":"60 ft."}],"auras":["destruction (30 ft.)"],"ac":18,"ac_touch":9,"ac_flat_footed":18,"ac_mods":"+9 natural, -1 size","hp":51,"hd":"6d10+18","hdvalue":6,"fort":8,"ref":5,"will":3,"defensive_abilities":["barbed defense"],"dr":"5/good or silver","immunities":["acid","death effects","disease","poison"],"resists":[{"type":"cold","value":10},{"type":"electricity","value":10},{"type":"fire","value":10}],"speed":[{"type":"land","speed":30},{"type":"fly","speed":30,"maneuverability":"average"}],"melee":"bite +10 (1d8+4), 2 claws +10 (1d6+4 plus bleed 1d4), tail slap +4 (1d8+2 plus bleed 1d4)","ranged":"4 shrapnel +5 (1d6+4/19-20)","space":"10 ft.","reach":"10 ft.","special_attacks":[{"type":"penetrating slivers"},{"type":"trample","details":"1d6+6 plus bleed 1d4, DC 17"}],"spell_like_abilities_caster_level":6,"spell_like_abilities_concentration":9,"spell_like_abilities":[{"frequency":"constant","spells":[{"spell":"detect good"}]},{"frequency":"at-will","spells":[{"spell":"cause fear"},{"spell":"lesser confusion"},{"spell":"message"},{"spell":"obscuring mist"}]},{"frequency":"1/day","spells":[{"spell":"crushing despair"},{"spell":"dispel magic"},{"spell":"meld into stone"},{"spell":"move earth"}]}],"strength":18,"dexterity":11,"constitution":16,"intelligence":11,"wisdom":12,"charisma":15,"baseatk":6,"cmb":11,"cmd":21,"feats":[{"feat":"Power Attack"},{"feat":"Weapon Focus (bite)"},{"feat":"Weapon Focus (claws)"}],"skills":[{"skill":"Fly","value":7},{"skill":"Intimidate","value":11},{"skill":"Knowledge (engineering)","value":9},{"skill":"Knowledge (planes)","value":9},{"skill":"Perception","value":10},{"skill":"Stealth","value":5}],"languages":["Abyssal","Draconic","Infernal","telepathy 100 ft."],"environments":["any (Abaddon)"],"organizations":["solitary","squad (2-18)"],"treasure":"standard","description_visual":"Jagged bits of metal, armor, and sharp pieces of wire embed the flesh of this towering fiend.","group":"Daemon","source":"AP 71","special_abilities":[{"type":"Aura of Destruction","kind":"Su","details":"A genthodaemon can create an aura of pure carnage. All critical threats made against targets within the aura (including the genthodaemon) are automatically confirmed. Dying creatures within the aura take a -10 penalty on stabilization checks. The genthodaemon can activate or suppress this aura as a free action."},{"type":"Barbed Defense","kind":"Su","details":"A creature that strikes a genthodaemon with a melee weapon, an unarmed strike, or a natural weapon takes 1d4+4 points of piercing damage from the barbed wire and other pieces of jagged metal embedded in the genthodaemon's body. Melee weapons with reach don't endanger their users in this way."},{"type":"Penetrating Slivers","kind":"Ex","details":"When a genthodaemon confirms a critical hit with a claw attack, pieces of its metal nails break off and enter the target's body, working their way toward its heart. When the slivers reach the heart 1d3 rounds later, the creature takes 1d6 points of Constitution damage. The slivers are destroyed by anything that removes curses, diseases, or death effects. Likewise, creatures immune to curses, diseases, and death effects are immune to this ability."},{"type":"Shrapnel","kind":"Ex","details":"A genthodaemon can shake loose four large pieces of the shrapnel embedded in its body as a standard action (make a separate attack roll for each piece). This attack has a range of 180 ft. with no range increment. All targets of this attack must be within 30 ft. of each other. The daemon can launch at most 24 pieces of shrapnel in any 24-hour period."}],"description":"Genthodaemons are common troops of daemonic armies, resolutely obedient to any greater type of daemon that gives them orders. They personify death in hopeless or futile wars, genocide, and the despair created by long, bloody stalemates where the combatants lose their will to live and forget why they were fighting in the first place. They have almost no role in corrupting mortals, as they are devoid of interest in the fates of most other creatures, but are sometimes called by daemonologists or greater daemons for use in war or their ability to shape battlefields. Any daemon that can summon a ceustodaemon can instead use its summon ability to summon a genthodaemon.\n\nA genthodaemon looks like a stereotypical fiend- basically humanoid, but with claws, a tail, batlike wings, and cloven hooves. Metal armored plates, barbs, and spikes cover its body, though these are part of the daemon rather than armor it wears. Its claws are jagged metal shards sprouting from its fingers where nails should be.\n\nGenthodaemons are only slightly above cacodaemons and lacridaemons in the hierarchy of Abaddon. A greater daemon may create a genthodaemon from a cacodaemon or one of the hunted (a dead soul trying to survive on Abaddon); however, most arise naturally from war-battered souls who band together as hunted, transforming into true daemons simultaneously when the group has cannibalized enough souls. Genthodaemons show unusual loyalty to others in their band, though this doesn't interfere with their obligations to more powerful daemons.\n\nA typical genthodaemon stands over 9 ft. tall and weighs 500 to 600 pounds (with much of this weight stemming from the daemon's embedded metal).\n\nEcology\n\nSouls that become genthodaemons usually come from worlds where war technology has advanced to allow production of large amounts of metal armor and weapons-particularly worlds where firearms have been invented. When battle grows so such a scope that the enemy becomes a faceless tide, or killing becomes casual and easy at long range, the act of waging war becomes completely dehumanized and soldiers become mere pieces in a perpetual machine. In such grim instances and the seeds are planted to send soldiers' souls to Abaddon.\n\nThese ties to the craft of war stain the dead soul and the daemon created from it, manifesting as armor plates fused with daemonic flesh, pieces of weapons embedded in its bones, or even remnants of siege engines or barbed wire sprouting from or wrapped around the daemon's body. These elements are part of the daemon, not mere decorations, but any mechanical pieces merely resemble functional items and no longer work (for example, a daemon with a crossbow or rifle embedded in its arm cannot shoot it). Genthodaemons from the same band often resemble each other, including the shape of their metal parts, sometimes because their mortal selves were even in the same army and uniform.\n\nThough genthodaemons serve in the armies of all four Horsemen, they are most strongly associated with Szuriel, the Horseman of War. Her military background, strong discipline, and focus on the brutality of war resonates with the core of a genthodaemon's being. Those who (as mortals) were involved in acts of genocide have a morbid fascination with obscisidaemons and tend to follow them. Though genthodaemons were not the instigators of genocide in their mortal lives (which could have granted them higher status on Abaddon), those who participated in such acts feel a fawning admiration for the greater daemons who orchestrated such atrocities.\n\nLike other daemons, genthodaemons hate all living things-and to an extent, themselves-and look forward to the death of the last mortal, for on that day they will have no other reminders of their bleak mortal lives and can focus all their hate inward.\n\nHabitat & Society\n\nGenthodaemons patrol the fortresses, cities, ruins, and wastelands of Abaddon looking for invaders, hostile non-daemons, and gangs of the hunted. Because they usually travel in groups, they are rarely preyed upon by other creatures and only have to face death when deployed as part of a Horseman's army. They are of low status but fulfill a necessary role as soldiers of Abaddon. Greater daemons treat them with the formal respect due their relative difference in rank, much like how a general might treat a common soldier. However, in the wars against mortal life, the Horsemen understand the value of suicidal missions and brazen sacrifice, and are not above sending countless genthodaemons to permanent destruction if it advances the cause of Abaddon. The genthodaemons accept this as their lot and never complain, as protests require effort, will, and the belief that there is a possibility of change-three things these shell-shocked creatures lost long ago.\n\nOn the Material Plane, genthodaemons sometimes serve daemonic cults that are unworthy of a greater daemon's attention or lacking the power to summon a more powerful creature. As their magic is suitable for war and destruction, they have limited use to mortal cultists not intent on violence.","ecology":"Souls that become genthodaemons usually come from worlds where war technology has advanced to allow production of large amounts of metal armor and weapons-particularly worlds where firearms have been invented. When battle grows so such a scope that the enemy becomes a faceless tide, or killing becomes casual and easy at long range, the act of waging war becomes completely dehumanized and soldiers become mere pieces in a perpetual machine. In such grim instances and the seeds are planted to send soldiers' souls to Abaddon.\n\nThese ties to the craft of war stain the dead soul and the daemon created from it, manifesting as armor plates fused with daemonic flesh, pieces of weapons embedded in its bones, or even remnants of siege engines or barbed wire sprouting from or wrapped around the daemon's body. These elements are part of the daemon, not mere decorations, but any mechanical pieces merely resemble functional items and no longer work (for example, a daemon with a crossbow or rifle embedded in its arm cannot shoot it). Genthodaemons from the same band often resemble each other, including the shape of their metal parts, sometimes because their mortal selves were even in the same army and uniform.\n\nThough genthodaemons serve in the armies of all four Horsemen, they are most strongly associated with Szuriel, the Horseman of War. Her military background, strong discipline, and focus on the brutality of war resonates with the core of a genthodaemon's being. Those who (as mortals) were involved in acts of genocide have a morbid fascination with obscisidaemons and tend to follow them. Though genthodaemons were not the instigators of genocide in their mortal lives (which could have granted them higher status on Abaddon), those who participated in such acts feel a fawning admiration for the greater daemons who orchestrated such atrocities.\n\nLike other daemons, genthodaemons hate all living things-and to an extent, themselves-and look forward to the death of the last mortal, for on that day they will have no other reminders of their bleak mortal lives and can focus all their hate inward.","habitat_society":"Genthodaemons patrol the fortresses, cities, ruins, and wastelands of Abaddon looking for invaders, hostile non-daemons, and gangs of the hunted. Because they usually travel in groups, they are rarely preyed upon by other creatures and only have to face death when deployed as part of a Horseman's army. They are of low status but fulfill a necessary role as soldiers of Abaddon. Greater daemons treat them with the formal respect due their relative difference in rank, much like how a general might treat a common soldier. However, in the wars against mortal life, the Horsemen understand the value of suicidal missions and brazen sacrifice, and are not above sending countless genthodaemons to permanent destruction if it advances the cause of Abaddon. The genthodaemons accept this as their lot and never complain, as protests require effort, will, and the belief that there is a possibility of change-three things these shell-shocked creatures lost long ago.\n\nOn the Material Plane, genthodaemons sometimes serve daemonic cults that are unworthy of a greater daemon's attention or lacking the power to summon a more powerful creature. As their magic is suitable for war and destruction, they have limited use to mortal cultists not intent on violence."},{"name":"Ghul","cr":5,"xp":1600,"alignment":"CE","size":"Medium","type":"undead","subtypes":["shapechanger"],"init":2,"senses":[{"type":"darkvision","modifier":"60 ft."}],"ac":18,"ac_touch":12,"ac_flat_footed":16,"ac_mods":"+2 Dex, +6 natural","hp":51,"hd":"6d8+24","hdvalue":6,"fort":8,"ref":4,"will":7,"dr":"5/good","immunities":["undead traits"],"resists":[{"type":"fire","value":10}],"speed":[{"type":"land","speed":40},{"type":"climb","speed":20}],"melee":"bite +10 (1d6+6), 2 claws +10 (1d4+6 plus bleed)","space":"5 ft.","reach":"5 ft.","special_attacks":[{"type":"bleed","details":"1"},{"type":"cursed claws"},{"type":"rend","details":"2 claws, 1d6+6"}],"strength":22,"dexterity":15,"intelligence":14,"wisdom":15,"charisma":18,"baseatk":4,"cmb":10,"cmd":22,"feats":[{"feat":"Great Fortitude"},{"feat":"Power Attack"},{"feat":"Step Up"}],"skills":[{"skill":"Bluff","value":10},{"skill":"Climb","value":18},{"skill":"Diplomacy","value":7},{"skill":"Disguise","value":11},{"skill":"Intimidate","value":11},{"skill":"Perception","value":15},{"skill":"Stealth","value":11},{"skill":"Survival","value":8,"special":"+16 when following tracks"}],"languages":["Common","one elemental language (Aquan, Auran, Ignan, or Terran)","one planar language (Abyssal, Celestial, or Infernal)"],"sq":[{"type":"change shape","details":"hyena; doesn't detect as undead in this form; beast shape I"},{"type":"genie-kin"}],"environments":["warm deserts"],"organizations":["solitary","pack (2-8)"],"treasure":"standard","description_visual":"Draped in fine dark robes, this undead creature's body seems to be made of equal parts bone, leathery flesh, and blood-red smoke.","source":"PFRPG Bestiary 3","special_abilities":[{"type":"Cursed Claws","kind":"Ex","details":"A ghul's claws count as both cold iron and magic for the purpose of bypassing damage reduction."},{"type":"Genie-kin","kind":"Ex","details":"For all race-related effects (such as a ranger's favored enemy), a ghul is considered a genie even though its type is undead."}],"description":"Ghuls are undead jann whose eternal existence was twisted by fate and wrought through the displeasure of Ahriman, Lord of the Divs. As if the curse of undeath and ravenous hunger were not enough, these once-majestic creatures now bear donkey hooves as ft. Despite their horrific undead appearance, this feature shames them the most, and they hide their feet from view.\n\nGhuls, like ghouls and ghasts, haunt cemeteries and other places of the dead hoping to feed on corpses. They also hunt mourners and grave tenders, as they enjoy the taste of living prey as well as that of the dead. Selective in their diets, ghuls choose their victims by personality, believing innocence and youth taste more delicious than the barely palatable flesh of the bitter and old. Sometimes a ghul follows a funeral procession in hyena form, keeping a safe distance until the ceremony, whereupon it changes into its true form to attack and feast. Sorrow and despair taste as delicious as innocence to a ghul.\n\nWhile not directly affected by sunlight, ghuls despise its presence and only move about during the day if forced to by necessity. They primarily hunt at night, sometimes straying far from their graveyard lairs and burial caves in search of fresh prey to sate their hunger.\n\nThe longer a ghul goes without feeding, the more ferocious and primal the creature becomes. A well-sated ghul organizes with others of its kind and lesser undead, tormenting nearby towns and settlements. A ghul involved with this level of organization often has a scattered set of lairs throughout the desert. These allow the ghul to strike far from its home lair and hide again without having to travel during the blistering daylight sun. When a ghul goes for too long without feeding, it becomes increasingly feral and violent-its statistics don't change, but it grows less concerned with fleeing combat, even when it is obviously outmatched.\n\nGhuls stand 6 ft. tall and weigh 90 pounds.","ecology":"","habitat_society":""},{"name":"Giant Ant Lion","cr":5,"xp":1600,"alignment":"N","size":"Large","type":"vermin","init":0,"senses":[{"type":"darkvision","modifier":"60 ft."},{"type":"tremorsense","modifier":"60 ft."}],"ac":19,"ac_touch":9,"ac_flat_footed":19,"ac_mods":"+10 natural, -1 size","hp":60,"hd":"8d8+24","hdvalue":8,"fort":9,"ref":2,"will":2,"immunities":["mind-affecting effects"],"speed":[{"type":"land","speed":30},{"type":"burrow","speed":10}],"melee":"bite +10 (2d8+7 plus grab)","space":"10 ft.","reach":"5 ft.","special_attacks":[{"type":"sand trap"}],"strength":20,"dexterity":11,"constitution":17,"wisdom":11,"charisma":10,"baseatk":6,"cmb":12,"cmb_special":"+4 vs. grapple","cmd":22,"cmd_special":"+8 vs. trip","environments":["warm deserts"],"organizations":["solitary","nest (2-4)"],"treasure":"incidental","description_visual":"This gigantic burrowing insect has an oversized abdomen. A terrifying set of long, hooked mandibles protrudes from its head.","group":"Giant","source":"PFRPG Bestiary 3","special_abilities":[{"type":"Sand Trap","kind":"Ex","details":"A giant ant lion can create a 60-foot-diameter, 20-foot-deep pit in any sand or soft earth surface. Creating a sand trap takes 1 hour. A DC 15 Perception check allows a creature to realize such a depression in the sand is in fact a trap. Any creature that steps into the trap slides to the center if it fails a DC 14 Reflex save-such victims take no damage, but they do fall prone. A giant ant lion can make an attack of opportunity against any creature that falls to the bottom of its sand trap. These creatures can move across sand traps at their normal speed and are immune to the trap's effects. Other creatures can navigate the trap's walls with a DC 20 Climb check."}],"description":"Perils of the desert depths, giant ant lions construct traps from the shifting sands. These beasts lurk at the base of these pits, half buried and patiently awaiting unwary prey.","ecology":"","habitat_society":""},{"name":"Giant Blowfish","cr":5,"xp":1600,"alignment":"N","size":"Large","type":"animal","subtypes":["aquatic"],"init":5,"senses":[{"type":"low-light vision"}],"ac":18,"ac_touch":10,"ac_flat_footed":17,"ac_mods":"+8 armor, +1 Dex, -1 size, +0 natural","hp":52,"hd":"7d8+21","hdvalue":7,"fort":10,"ref":6,"will":4,"defensive_abilities":["quills"],"speed":[{"type":"land","speed":30},{"type":"swim","speed":30}],"melee":"1 slam +9 (1d8+4 plus poison)","space":"10 ft.","reach":"10 ft.","strength":18,"dexterity":13,"constitution":17,"intelligence":1,"wisdom":14,"charisma":12,"baseatk":5,"cmb":10,"cmd":21,"cmd_special":"can't be tripped","feats":[{"feat":"Great Fortitude"},{"feat":"Improved Initiative"},{"feat":"Skill Focus (Perception)"},{"feat":"Weapon Focus (slam)"}],"skills":[{"skill":"Perception","value":5},{"skill":"Swim","value":14}],"environments":["warm oceans"],"organizations":["solitary","pair","school (3-9)"],"treasure":"none","description_visual":"A blowfish the size of a horse drifts through the water, its quills the size of spears.","group":"Fish","source":"AP 56","special_abilities":[{"type":"Poison","kind":"Ex","details":"Slam aand quills-injury; save Fort DC 16; frequency 1/ round for 6 rounds; effect 1d6 Dex, prevents breath holding; cure 1 save. Giant blowfish venom causes violent seizing of the muscles. Any non-aquatic creature affected must immediately begin making Constitution checks to continue holding its breath. The save DC is Constitution-based."},{"type":"Quills","kind":"Ex","details":"Any creature attacking a giant blowfish with light or one-handed melee weapons, natural weapons, or an unarmed strike takes 1d8 points of piercing damage. A creature that grapples a giant blowfish takes 2d6 points of piercing damage each round it does so. Creatures damaged by a giant blowfish's quills must also save or be poisoned. These gigantic cousins of common blowfish, covered in poisonous spearlike quills, can reach up to 10 ft. in size, and nearly double that when fully inf lated."}],"description":"A gigantic, prehistoric fish cuts through the water, its head like a snapping turtle, complete with sharp, toothlike plates.","ecology":"","habitat_society":""},{"name":"Giant Crawling Hand","cr":5,"xp":1600,"alignment":"NE","size":"Medium","type":"undead","init":2,"senses":[{"type":"blindsense","modifier":"30 ft."},{"type":"darkvision","modifier":"60 ft."}],"ac":19,"ac_touch":13,"ac_flat_footed":16,"ac_mods":"+2 Dex, +1 dodge, +6 natural","hp":52,"hd":"7d8+21","hdvalue":7,"fort":4,"ref":6,"will":6,"immunities":["undead traits"],"speed":[{"type":"land","speed":30}],"melee":"claw +11 (1d6+7 plus grab)","space":"5 ft.","reach":"5 ft.","special_attacks":[{"type":"mark quarry"},{"type":"pus burst"}],"strength":21,"dexterity":15,"intelligence":2,"wisdom":13,"charisma":14,"baseatk":5,"cmb":10,"cmb_special":"+4 vs. grapple","cmd":23,"feats":[{"feat":"Dodge"},{"feat":"Lightning Reflexes"},{"feat":"Toughness"},{"feat":"Weapon Focus (claw)"}],"skills":[{"skill":"Acrobatics","value":3},{"skill":"Perception","value":7},{"skill":"Stealth","value":12}],"languages":["Common (can't speak)"],"environments":["any land","any underground"],"organizations":["solitary","gang (2-5)"],"treasure":"none","description_visual":"A giant rotting hand rocks unsteadily, propped upon thick, stubby fingers. Its pale, necrotic flesh pulses and crawls with sickly boils.","group":"Crawling Hand","source":"PFRPG Bestiary 2","special_abilities":[{"type":"Mark Quarry","kind":"Su","details":"A crawling hand is assigned a quarry by anointing the hand with a drop of the intended quarry's blood. If the hand has no current quarry, it automatically gains the next creature it damages as its quarry. Once attuned to a target, it becomes aware of the target's location as if under the effect of a continuous locate creature spell. The hand gains a +1 bonus on all attack rolls, damage rolls, and skill checks made to seek out and destroy the marked quarry. The mark quarry ability lasts until the quarry or the hand is slain."},{"type":"Pus Burst","kind":"Su","details":"When damaged by a piercing or slashing melee weapon, a spray of vile pus strikes the attacker unless the attacker makes a DC 15 Reflex save. Weapons that provide reach protect the attacker completely from a pus burst. Creatures struck by pus become nauseated for 1d3 rounds and take 2d6 points of negative energy damage. The save DC is Charisma-based."}],"description":"Some say the origins of the crawling hand lie in the experiments of demented necromancers contracted to construct tiny assassins. Other tales tell of gruesome prosthetics sparked to life by evil magic, which then developed primitive sentience and vengefully strangled their hosts. Regardless, the crawling hand is an efficient killing tool.\n\nWhen not commanded to kill, the crawling hand remains still and can be handled and transported safely.\n\nTypically, owners carry them about in small, velvet-lined boxes. Crawling hands can also be placed as guardians and programmed with contingency commands, such as to hunt down and slay an individual who performs a specific task like violating a shrine, opening a warded door, or breaking the seal of a casket.","ecology":"","habitat_society":""},{"name":"Giant Frilled Lizard","cr":5,"xp":1600,"alignment":"N","size":"Large","type":"animal","init":5,"senses":[{"type":"low-light vision"},{"type":"scent"}],"ac":18,"ac_touch":10,"ac_flat_footed":17,"ac_mods":"+1 Dex, +8 natural, -1 size","hp":59,"hd":"7d8+28","hdvalue":7,"fort":11,"ref":8,"will":4,"speed":[{"type":"land","speed":30},{"type":"climb","speed":30}],"melee":"bite +9 (2d6+5), tail +4 (1d8+2)","space":"10 ft.","reach":"5 ft.","special_attacks":[{"type":"intimidating charge"}],"strength":21,"dexterity":13,"constitution":19,"intelligence":2,"wisdom":14,"charisma":10,"baseatk":5,"cmb":11,"cmd":22,"cmd_special":"+4 vs. trip","feats":[{"feat":"Great Fortitude"},{"feat":"Improved Initiative"},{"feat":"Lightning Reflexes"},{"feat":"Skill Focus (Perception)"}],"skills":[{"skill":"Climb","value":13},{"skill":"Perception","value":11},{"skill":"Stealth","value":8}],"environments":["any hills","any plains","warm forests"],"organizations":["solitary","pair","pack (3-8)"],"treasure":"none","description_visual":"This bright-eyed lizard is larger than a horse. As it hisses in anger, a brightly colored frill extends around its neck.","group":"Lizard","source":"PFRPG Bestiary","special_abilities":[{"type":"Intimidating Charge","kind":"Ex","details":"When a giant frilled lizard charges, it hisses ferociously, extends its neck frills, and darts forward on its hind legs, increasing its base speed to 50 ft. for that round. In addition to the normal effects of a charge, the creature charged must make a DC 13 Will save or be shaken for 1d6 rounds. This is a fear effect. The save DC is Charisma-based."}],"description":"This lizard is a true behemoth- an apex predator found in tropical regions. Many species of giant lizard exist-other species lack this lizard's intimidating charge special ability but might have other special attacks like grab, trip, constrict (with a bite), or pounce.","ecology":"","habitat_society":""},{"name":"Giant Hellgrammite","cr":5,"xp":1600,"alignment":"N","size":"Large","type":"vermin","subtypes":["aquatic"],"init":0,"senses":[{"type":"darkvision","modifier":"60 ft."}],"ac":16,"ac_touch":9,"ac_flat_footed":16,"ac_mods":"+7 natural, -1 size","hp":52,"hd":"7d8+21","hdvalue":7,"fort":8,"ref":2,"will":1,"immunities":["mind-affecting effects"],"speed":[{"type":"land","speed":30},{"type":"swim","speed":20}],"melee":"bite +8 (2d8+4 plus grab), tail slap +3 (1d8+2)","space":"10 ft.","reach":"10 ft.","special_attacks":[{"type":"death grip"},{"type":"constrict","details":"2d8+4"}],"strength":19,"dexterity":11,"constitution":16,"wisdom":9,"charisma":2,"baseatk":5,"cmb":10,"cmb_special":"+4 vs. grapple","cmd":20,"cmd_special":"+8 vs. trip","skills":[{"skill":"Climb","value":8},{"skill":"Perception","value":5,"special":"+9 in water"},{"skill":"Stealth","value":1,"special":"+9 in water"},{"skill":"Swim","value":7}],"sq":[{"type":"amphibious"}],"environments":["temperate rivers","temperate streams"],"organizations":["solitary","pair"],"treasure":"none","description_visual":"More than a dozen segmented black legs propel this giant, caterpillar-like insect, its thick head dominated by a pair of powerful, gnashing pincers.","source":"AP 49","special_abilities":[{"type":"Death Grip","kind":"Ex","details":"Upon its death, a hellgrammite's massive jaws close tightly and lock. Any creature or object that is grappled at the time of the hellgrammite's death takes an additional 1d8+2 points of damage per round unless it makes a DC 22 Escape Artist check, makes a DC 20 Strength check, or deals an additional 10 points of damage to the hellgrammite's corpse to escape."}],"description":"Giant hellgrammites are the fierce and predacious larvae of dobsonf lies of terrifying size. While their diminutive brethren measure several inches long, the monstrous variety measure 10 ft. long and weigh up to 800 pounds.\n\nThe body of the giant hellgrammite is long and flattened, and generally ranges from dark brown to reddish in color.\n\nThe front third of its body consists of a well-armored head and thoracic segments, with stout, segmented legs. The head possesses a fearsome set of jaws, small antennae, and a set of clustered eye spots on either side. The posterior twothirds of the hellgrammite consist of nine densely haired abdominal segments, each with a pair of lateral filaments and gill tufts underneath for underwater breathing. The hellgrammite's abdomen ends in pair of terminal prolegs, each with a pair of hooks, which aid the creature in climbing or anchoring itself in fast-moving currents, and can be used in a tail slap to slash at its enemies.\n\nThe giant hellgrammite is a simple but ferocious predator. It latches onto its prey with its deadly jaws and proceeds to crush the life from it, feeding on the body after its victim can no longer struggle. The hellgrammite often attempts to drag the prey back to a protected area between or behind stream boulders, or down to the bottom of a deep side-pool. The strength of a hellgrammite's jaws is so great that, even in death, they clench tightly and don't relinquish their grip.","ecology":"","habitat_society":""},{"name":"Giant Moray Eel","cr":5,"xp":1600,"alignment":"N","size":"Large","type":"animal","subtypes":["aquatic"],"init":6,"senses":[{"type":"low-light vision"},{"type":"scent"}],"ac":19,"ac_touch":11,"ac_flat_footed":17,"ac_mods":"+2 Dex, +8 natural, -1 size","hp":52,"hd":"7d8+21","hdvalue":7,"fort":8,"ref":9,"will":3,"speed":[{"type":"swim","speed":30}],"melee":"bite +11 (2d6+9 plus grab)","space":"10 ft.","reach":"10 ft.","special_attacks":[{"type":"gnaw"}],"strength":22,"dexterity":14,"constitution":16,"intelligence":1,"wisdom":12,"charisma":8,"baseatk":5,"cmb":12,"cmb_special":"+4 vs. grapple","cmd":24,"cmd_special":"can't be tripped","feats":[{"feat":"Improved Initiative"},{"feat":"Lightning Reflexes"},{"feat":"Skill Focus (Stealth)"},{"feat":"Weapon Focus (bite)"}],"skills":[{"skill":"Escape Artist","value":10},{"skill":"Perception","value":7},{"skill":"Stealth","value":8},{"skill":"Swim","value":14}],"environments":["warm oceans"],"organizations":["solitary","pair","nest (3-6)"],"treasure":"none","description_visual":"This sixteen-foot-long eel slithers through the water with uncanny grace, mouth open to display large teeth and a second set of jaws.","group":"Eel","source":"PFRPG Bestiary","special_abilities":[{"type":"Gnaw","kind":"Ex","details":"If a giant moray begins a round with a grabbed foe, it inflicts automatic bite damage (2d6+9 points of damage). A giant moray eel possesses a second set of jaws in its throat that aid in swallowing-it can make a second bite attack (+11 attack, 1d6+3) against a foe it has already grabbed."}],"description":"The moray eel is a ferocious predator, capable of holding prey in its primary jaws while a smaller set of jaws inside its throat chews away bite-sized portions. Moray Eel Animal Companion Starting Statistics: Size Medium; AC +5 natural armor; Speed swim 30 ft.; Attack bite (1d8); Ability Scores Str 14, Dex 16, Con 12, Int 1, Wis 12, Cha 8; Special Qualities low-light vision, grab. 7th-Level Advancement: Size Large; AC +3 natural armor; Attack bite (2d6); Ability Scores Str +8, Dex -2, Con +4; Special Qualities gnaw.","ecology":"","habitat_society":""},{"name":"Giant Owl","cr":5,"xp":1600,"alignment":"LN","size":"Huge","type":"magical beast","init":5,"senses":[{"type":"low-light vision"}],"ac":18,"ac_touch":10,"ac_flat_footed":16,"ac_mods":"+2 Dex, +8 natural, -2 size","hp":57,"hd":"6d10+24","hdvalue":6,"fort":9,"ref":7,"will":5,"speed":[{"type":"land","speed":10},{"type":"fly","speed":60,"maneuverability":"average"}],"melee":"2 claws +9 (2d6+5)","space":"15 ft.","reach":"10 ft.","strength":20,"dexterity":15,"constitution":18,"intelligence":14,"wisdom":17,"charisma":9,"baseatk":6,"cmb":13,"cmb_special":"+2 vs. disarm","cmd":25,"cmd_special":"+2 vs. disarm","feats":[{"feat":"Alertness"},{"feat":"Combat Expertise"},{"feat":"Improved Disarm"}],"skills":[{"skill":"Fly","value":11},{"skill":"Knowledge (geography, history, nature)","value":11},{"skill":"Perception","value":17},{"skill":"Sense Motive","value":15},{"skill":"Stealth","value":9}],"languages":["Sylvan (cannot speak)","animal telepathy (30 ft.)"],"sq":[{"type":"insightful senses"},{"type":"piercing stare"}],"environments":["temperate forests"],"organizations":["solitary","pair","roost (3-10)"],"treasure":"none","description_visual":"This enormous owl has a silent gracefulness and wisdom about it despite its considerable size.","group":"Owl","source":"PFRPG Bestiary 3","special_abilities":[{"type":"Animal Telepathy","kind":"Su","details":"A giant owl can telepathically communicate with other animals as if under the effects of speak with animals."},{"type":"Insightful Senses","kind":"Su","details":"A giant owl's senses are particularly well honed to sensing danger or locating prey. It adds its Wisdom modifier as an insight bonus (+3 for most giant owls) on its initiative checks."},{"type":"Piercing Stare","kind":"Su","details":"Up to three times per day as a swift action, a giant owl can gain the effects of true seeing (as the spell) for 1 round."}],"description":"The noble giant owl roosts in the tallest trees or in high caves near its forest home. No mere animals, giant owls pride themselves as intellectuals and keen judges of character. Little passes a giant owl's notice in its home forest. What it doesn't observe directly, it learns from its wide network of animal informants. Giant owls view themselves more as watchers than participants, rarely interfering in the affairs of others barring a threat to their homes. Giant owls stand 18 ft. tall and have 30-foot wingspans, yet they rarely weigh over 600 pounds.","ecology":"","habitat_society":""},{"name":"Giant Queen Bee","cr":5,"xp":1600,"alignment":"N","size":"Large","type":"vermin","init":0,"senses":[{"type":"darkvision","modifier":"60 ft."}],"ac":17,"ac_touch":9,"ac_flat_footed":17,"ac_mods":"+8 natural, -1 size","hp":59,"hd":"7d8+28","hdvalue":7,"fort":9,"ref":2,"will":3,"immunities":["mindaffecting effects"],"weaknesses":["vulnerable to smoke"],"speed":[{"type":"land","speed":20},{"type":"fly","speed":60,"maneuverability":"good"}],"melee":"sting +8 (1d8+6 plus poison)","space":"10 ft.","reach":"5 ft.","strength":19,"dexterity":11,"constitution":18,"wisdom":12,"charisma":13,"baseatk":5,"cmb":10,"cmd":20,"cmd_special":"+8 vs. trip","skills":[{"skill":"Fly","value":2}],"environments":["temperate plains","warm plains"],"organizations":["solitary","colony (1 queen and 3-18 giant bees)"],"treasure":"incidental (royal jelly)","description_visual":"This giant bee is as large as a horse and unusually fat, with light brown stripes on her body.","group":"Bee","source":"PFRPG Bestiary 2","special_abilities":[{"type":"Poison","kind":"Ex","details":"Sting-injury; save Fort DC 17; frequency 1/round for 4 rounds; effect 1d2 Con; cure 2 consecutive saves."}],"description":"Bulbous and robust in comparison to others of the same species, giant queen bees are immense, fertile females, easily identified by their larger size and lighter stripes. They grow to 12 ft. in length and weigh 140 pounds. Royal Jelly When a hive of bees needs a new queen to replace one that has died, they feed specially selected larvae royal jelly to trigger the larvae's transformation into new queens. The first queen to hatch then kills any other new queens. Royal jelly has remarkable effects on other creatures if eaten. A pound of royal jelly provides enough nourishment for a full day, and grants a +4 resistance bonus on all saving throws against disease for the next 24 hours. If a creature sleeps enough to heal damage within 24 hours of eating at least a pound of royal jelly, it heals twice the hit points and ability damage as it otherwise would. A typical bee hive contains 2d6 pounds of royal jelly-a single pound sells for 100 gp.","ecology":"","habitat_society":""},{"name":"Giant Walking Stick","cr":5,"xp":1600,"alignment":"N","size":"Large","type":"vermin","init":2,"senses":[{"type":"darkvision","modifier":"60 ft."}],"ac":17,"ac_touch":11,"ac_flat_footed":15,"ac_mods":"+2 Dex, +6 natural, -1 size","hp":60,"hd":"8d8+24","hdvalue":8,"fort":9,"ref":4,"will":2,"immunities":["mind-affecting effects"],"speed":[{"type":"land","speed":30}],"melee":"2 slams +10 (1d6+5)","space":"10 ft.","reach":"10 ft.","special_attacks":[{"type":"nauseating spray"}],"strength":21,"dexterity":15,"constitution":17,"wisdom":10,"charisma":2,"baseatk":6,"cmb":12,"cmd":24,"cmd_special":"+8 vs. trip","skills":[{"skill":"Stealth","value":6,"special":"+14 in brushy areas"}],"environments":["warm deserts","warm forests"],"organizations":["solitary","pair","brood (3-12)"],"treasure":"none","description_visual":"What at first appears to be a spindly, dead tree suddenly crawls from its camouflage, its wiry antenna twitching in the air.","group":"Walking Stick","source":"AP 81","special_abilities":[{"type":"Nauseating Spray","kind":"Ex","details":"A walking stick can spray a 15-foot cone of irritating, foul-smelling liquid once every 1d4 rounds. Creatures within this area must succeed at a DC 17 Reflex save or be sickened for 5 rounds. Alternatively, the walking stick can target a single creature within 30 ft. with a stream of this fluid. To use this version of its nauseating spray, the walking stick must succeed at a ranged touch attack (+5 for most walking sticks). If the target is hit, it must succeed at a DC 17 Fortitude save or become nauseated for 5 rounds; otherwise, it is sickened for 5 rounds. The save DC is Constitution-based."}],"description":"More common in rocky badlands than the great sandy dunes, stick bugs of all sizes are often mistaken for the fallen palm fronds or thorny leaf less trees found near oases and other spots in the desert where vegetation grows. Giant walking sticks often remain motionless for days at a time, waiting for the right prey to pass by before striking. When engaged with a foe too difficult to overtake, giant walking sticks flee from the fight. They usually expel their nauseating spray in these situations, but some can even break off their own limbs in order to escape, regrowing the missing arm or leg later once they're safe.\n\nA fully grown giant walking stick stretches from 6 to 10 ft. long, but typically weighs only 120 pounds.","ecology":"","habitat_society":""},{"name":"Gibbering Mouther","cr":5,"xp":1600,"alignment":"N","size":"Medium","type":"aberration","init":3,"senses":[{"type":"all-around vision"},{"type":"darkvision","modifier":"60 ft."}],"ac":19,"ac_touch":13,"ac_flat_footed":16,"ac_mods":"+3 Dex, +6 natural","hp":46,"hd":"4d8+28","hdvalue":4,"fort":8,"ref":4,"will":5,"defensive_abilities":["amorphous"],"dr":"5/bludgeoning","immunities":["critical hits","precision damage"],"speed":[{"type":"land","speed":10},{"type":"swim","speed":20}],"melee":"6 bites +7 (1d4 plus grab)","space":"5 ft.","reach":"5 ft.","special_attacks":[{"type":"blood drain"},{"type":"engulf","details":"6d4 damage plus 2 Con damage, AC 13, hp 4"},{"type":"gibbering"},{"type":"ground manipulation"},{"type":"spittle","details":"+6 ranged touch"}],"strength":10,"dexterity":17,"constitution":24,"intelligence":4,"wisdom":13,"charisma":12,"baseatk":3,"cmb":3,"cmb_special":"+4 vs. grapple","cmd":16,"cmd_special":"can't be tripped","feats":[{"feat":"Weapon Finesse"},{"feat":"Weapon Focus (bite)"}],"skills":[{"skill":"Perception","value":12},{"skill":"Swim","value":8}],"languages":["Aklo"],"environments":["any underground"],"organizations":["solitary"],"treasure":"standard","description_visual":"This horrid mass of eyes, mouths, and formless flesh stares in all directions, its countless maws yammering ceaselessly.","source":"PFRPG Bestiary","special_abilities":[{"type":"All-Around Vision","kind":"Ex","details":"A gibbering mouther sees in all directions at once. It cannot be flanked."},{"type":"Amorphous","kind":"Ex","details":"A gibbering mouther's body is malleable and shapeless. It is immune to precision damage (like sneak attacks) and critical hits, and can move through an area as small as one-quarter its space without squeezing or oneeighth its space when squeezing."},{"type":"Blood Drain","kind":"Ex","details":"On a successful grapple check after grabbing, several of the creature's mouths attach to its target. Each round it maintains its grapple, its mouths automatically deal 1d4 points of bite damage and 1 point of Constitution damage as it drains its victim's blood."},{"type":"Engulf","kind":"Ex","details":"This ability functions as swallow whole, except for the following changes. An engulfed creature is trapped in the gibbering mouther's body, where several of its mouths continue to feed and drain blood. A gibbering mouther can engulf only one foe of its size or smaller at a time. If an engulfed creature cuts its way free (the mouther's damage reduction still applies to those inside), the mouther simply flows together again and can still use its engulf attack."},{"type":"Gibbering","kind":"Su","details":"As a free action, a gibbering mouther can emit a cacophony of maddening sound. All creatures other than gibbering mouthers within 60 ft. must succeed on a DC 13 Will save or be confused for 1 round. This is a mindaffecting compulsion insanity effect. A creature that saves cannot be affected by the same mouther's gibbering for 24 hours. The save DC is Constitution-based."},{"type":"Ground Manipulation","kind":"Su","details":"At will as a standard action, a gibbering mouther can cause stone and earth under its body to grow soft and muddy. The ground remains muddy for 1 minute after the mouther moves off of the location. A gibbering mouther can move through these areas with ease, but other creatures treat them as difficult terrain."},{"type":"Spittle","kind":"Ex","details":"Each round as a free action, a gibbering mouther can emit a stream of acidic spittle at one target within 30 ft. On a successful attack, the target is blinded for 1d4 rounds unless he succeeds on a DC 18 Fortitude save. The save DC is Constitution-based."}],"description":"Disgusting, loathsome, and hungry-these are the only words that properly describe the gibbering mouther. A foul beast that lurks in underground caves, sewers, and nightmares, mouthers have no societal, ecological or religious significance other than their ability to drive those that listen to them mad. Some scholars believe that gibbering mouthers are a lesser variant of the much more dangerous shoggoth, while others theorize they are a punishment by some powerful being or deity inf licted on those who have offended it.","ecology":"","habitat_society":""}];
